        {
          "step_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s08",
          "type": "recap",
          "concept_ids": [
            "data.dataset_rows_columns",
            "data.categorical_numeric_types"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Tile Recap",
          "title": "What you've learned",
          "lede": "Two foundational ideas that every analysis step builds on.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s08.b01",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "text": "A dataset is a table of observations: each row is one record, each column is one variable recorded across all records. Before you can check, clean or analyse delivery data, you need to know that a row is one delivery and a column is one attribute of every delivery.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#tb5"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f1",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f2",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f3",
                  "data.dataset_rows_columns#f4"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s08.b02",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "text": "Before doing anything with a column, ask: can these values be meaningfully added or subtracted, or are they just labels for groups? A numerical variable supports arithmetic; a categorical variable's values are levels. A column stored as digits is not automatically numerical \u2014 zone_code is categorical because adding zone codes is meaningless.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#tb4"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f1",
                  "data.categorical_numeric_types#f2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "up_next",
              "block_id": "tile_data_shape_and_types.s08.b03",
              "title": "Coming up",
              "text": "Next you'll look at data quality: how to spot missing values, impossible numbers and inconsistent categories in a real dataset."
            }
          ],
